          { q: "How much can I realistically earn per month?", a: "Active UGC creators who work across multiple brands typically earn between $3,000 and $15,000 per month. The range depends on your production speed, niche, rate per video, and number of active clients. Creators combining per-video work with volume programs and one or two retainers tend to earn the most consistently. Income scales with reliability and the number of brand relationships you maintain." },
          { q: "What equipment do I need to get started?", a: "A modern smartphone with a decent camera, a ring light or window for natural lighting, a phone tripod for stable footage, a quiet room for clean audio, and a free editing app like CapCut. The total investment is under $100. Brands specifically want content that looks authentic, not studio-produced. Over-investing in gear early on can actually work against you by making your content look too polished." },
          { q: "How long does it take to land the first paid gig?", a: "With a solid portfolio of 5-10 sample videos and active applications across multiple channels, most creators land their first paid project within 2-4 weeks. The timeline shortens significantly if you create spec videos for specific brands and reach out directly. Creators who join an agency network or marketplace often get matched faster because the platform handles discovery." },
          { q: "Should I specialize in a niche or stay general?", a: "Start general, specialize as you grow. Your first 10-20 projects will teach you which niches suit your personality and production style. Once you identify a category where your content consistently performs well, lean into it. Specialists command higher rates because brands value proven experience in their specific market." },
          { q: "What is the difference between UGC and influencer marketing?", a: "As a UGC creator, you are paid for content production, not audience access. The content you produce is owned or licensed by the brand and distributed through their channels and ad accounts. Influencers are paid to post content on their own accounts, leveraging their follower base for reach. UGC does not require an audience. It requires production skill." },
